# Database migrations

MarzbanNext uses Alembic for schema migrations. Migrations must remain compatible
with SQLite, MySQL and MariaDB.

The current schema includes users and administrators, remote nodes, managed
inbounds and outbounds, ordered routing rules, host settings, node certificates
and the many-to-many assignment tables used by node configuration generation.

## Apply migrations

Run from the repository root:

```bash
./.venv/bin/python -m alembic upgrade head
```

PowerShell:

```powershell
.\.venv\Scripts\python.exe -m alembic upgrade head
```

The production Docker image runs `alembic upgrade head` before starting the
application, so normal container updates apply migrations automatically.

## Inspect migration state

```bash
python -m alembic current
python -m alembic heads
python -m alembic history
```

## Create a migration

```bash
python -m alembic revision -m "describe the schema change"
```

Use autogeneration only as a starting point:

```bash
python -m alembic revision --autogenerate -m "describe the schema change"
```

Review generated SQL, foreign keys, defaults, data backfills and downgrade
behavior manually. Do not edit a migration that may already have been applied;
create a new revision instead.

## Safety rules

- Back up the database and `/var/lib/marzban` before upgrading production.
- Stop extra application replicas before a migration.
- Do not run the same migration concurrently from multiple containers.
- Test both a fresh database and an upgrade from the previous release.
- Avoid database-specific SQL unless equivalent SQLite and MySQL/MariaDB paths
  are implemented.
- Keep the Alembic graph at one head unless a deliberate merge migration is
  being added.
